cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Do Semantic Operators Survive Query Rewriting? A Noise-Calibrated Audit of LLM-Powered Query Processing"
abstract: >-
  SQL guarantees that logically equivalent queries return the same result.
  Asking for products that are not laptops is the same as removing the
  laptops from all products. Semantic operators let a large language model
  (LLM) decide, for each record, whether a condition such as "the product is
  a laptop" is true and it is unclear whether the guarantee still holds. It
  is hard to test because an LLM can answer the same request differently on
  each run, so equivalent queries disagree even when nothing is wrong. We
  compare how much two equivalent queries disagree with each other against
  how much one query disagrees with itself when repeated. The difference
  which we call excess divergence needs no labeled data and is zero in
  expectation for interchangeable queries. We introduce a benchmark of seven
  core identities and four extensions of them, each a pair of equivalent
  queries built on six existing datasets from four domains. We run it on
  seven LLMs in a study of approximately 0.9 million LLM calls and nearly
  every identity is violated for nearly every LLM. For example, for the
  negation identity where the query asks for the opposite condition and
  flips the answer, the root-mean-square change in the probability that the
  LLM judges a condition to be true is 0.16 to 0.37 depending on the model.
  Similarly, for the symmetry identity where the query asks whether two
  records describe the same item and the order of the two records is
  swapped, it ranges from 0.15 to 0.21. When we switch reasoning on for
  three of the LLMs, they still violate nearly every identity, although most
  violations become smaller for the two that actually reason.
